# Line-ending policy (added 2026-08-29)
#
# WHY: this repo is edited on Windows (core.autocrlf=true, so the working copy is
# CRLF) and RUNS on Oracle Linux. Files reach the server two ways - `git pull` and
# `scp` - and only one of them converts line endings. On 2026-08-29 that produced a
# real split: four files landed on Oracle as LF, while src/autonomous/job_monitor.py
# landed as CRLF, because it was already CRLF in the Windows working copy when it was
# copied. Every md5 comparison passed, because the comparison normalised \r away
# first. The drift was invisible to the check that was supposed to catch it.
#
# For .py that is cosmetic. For .sh it is not: a shell script with CRLF fails on
# Linux with the famously unhelpful "\r: command not found" - the interpreter reads
# the carriage return as part of the command name.
#
# Declaring eol=lf makes git check these out as LF on EVERY platform, so what runs on
# Oracle is byte-identical to what is committed, whichever way it got there.
# History is not rewritten; this only affects checkout and add.

# Executed on Linux - CRLF here is a genuine break, not a cosmetic one.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf

# Source: keep byte-identical between the committed blob and the deployed file.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.cjs   text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.ts    text eol=lf

# Config and docs read by both sides.
*.json  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf

# Never touch these - conversion corrupts them.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.db    binary
*.mp4   binary
*.woff2 binary
